<p>Because he had to earn a living painting portraits, Thomas Gainsborough could not spend as much time sketching outdoors as he would have liked. So he arranged pieces of moss, rock, vegetables, and mirror into tabletop &#8220;landscapes&#8221; that he could paint at night by candlelight.</p>
<p>Joshua Reynolds recalled him collecting &#8220;broken stones, dried herbs, and pieces of looking glass, which he magnified and improved into rocks, trees, and water.&#8221; Another visitor wrote, &#8220;I &#8230; have more than once sat by him of an evening and seen him make models &#8230; for landscape scenery on a little old-fashioned folding oak table. &#8230; He would place cork or coal for his foregrounds; make middle grounds of sand and clay, bushes of moss and lichens, and set up distant woods of broccoli.&#8221;</p>
